    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i'm looking at some paperwork for a house i'm buying in seattle and it keeps mentioning \"escrow.\" i don't really get what that actually means or what the person in charge of it actually does. like why can't i just pay the seller directly?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow in a real estate transaction is like a temporary, secure holding place for your money and the property documents. It's a neutral third party, often a title company or an attorney, who manages the exchange between you and the seller. They hold your earnest money and other funds until all the conditions of the sale, like inspections and financing, are met. This prevents both parties from getting burned, which is much safer than just handing over cash to the seller directly.",
